Third-party models are NOT part of this repository and carry their own licences. The lines below say what each licence page said on
the day it was read and who read it. None of this is legal advice: read the pages yourself on the day you use them, because pages change.
Sources, revisions and the reader of each row: docs/licences.md.

- Chatterbox original, 500M (the recommended clone lane; compose profiles `chatterbox` and `chatterbox-cpu`, docker/chatterbox):
  the GitHub LICENSE file says MIT (read 2026-10-29); the Hugging Face page https://huggingface.co/ResembleAI/chatterbox shows the
  tag "mit" (read 2026-10-29). The card describes the training data only as "0.5M hours of cleaned data" and says "Prompts are
  sourced from freely available data on the internet", so its provenance is unknown.
  WATERMARK: every output carries Resemble's Perth implicit watermark (the resemble-perth package 1.0.1; its PyPI page says MIT
  License, read 2026-10-29), applied inside the library's generate() (read from the code of the pinned chatterbox-tts 0.1.7; no
  detection run on this project's output exists). This project keeps it on and never patches it out. The vendor describes it as
  imperceptible and says it survives MP3 compression (not tested here); you cannot set its payload. Do not describe such audio as
  watermark-free.
- OmniVoice (optional; compose profiles `gpu` and `cpu-clone`): the code page says Apache-2.0; the model card says the WEIGHTS are
  CC-BY-NC (non-commercial), read 2026-10-28. If your channel is or becomes monetized, or a sponsor or client is involved, that licence
  may not allow it. The page is silent on whether the licence covers generated audio.
- Kokoro-82M (CPU preset voices, no cloning; compose profile `cpu`, docker/kokoro): the model page says code and weights Apache-2.0
  (read 2026-10-28, re-read 2026-10-29), https://huggingface.co/hexgrad/Kokoro-82M. The card says the training data is
  "permissive/non-copyrighted" audio including "Synthetic audio generated by closed TTS models from large providers"
  (not checked here). Its training data includes CC BY audio that asks for credit:
  Koniwa `tnc` (CC BY 3.0, https://github.com/koniwa/koniwa) and SIWIS (CC BY 4.0, https://datashare.ed.ac.uk/handle/10283/2353).
  The rest of its training data was not checked here. Phonemes come from espeak-ng, whose README says GPL version 3 or later
  (read 2026-10-29); it is installed in that image, not in this repository, so a published copy of that image carries the GPL terms.
- WhisperX 3.7.9 (BSD-2-Clause), faster-whisper and Whisper small.en (MIT), wav2vec2-base-960h alignment model (Apache-2.0; trained on LibriSpeech,
  CC BY 4.0, Panayotov et al.), the AST audio tagger (BSD-3-Clause per its card), Transformers (Apache-2.0), PyTorch and torchaudio (BSD):
  read 2026-10-28, details and revisions in docs/licences.md.
- NOT read: the AudioSet data terms (the AST tagger was trained on it) and NVIDIA's terms for the proprietary CUDA runtime packages that
  the Linux CUDA PyTorch wheels pull in.
- libsndfile (inside the soundfile wheel) is LGPL-2.1: installed in the tools image, not part of this repository. A published image
